    description: "FILED 2026-09-09 from WI-garar stage 2's A/B, which it refuted. Record:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/garar2_objc_09092026/RESULT.md.\n\ntree-sitter-objc FAILS TO PARSE a large minority of real Objective-C headers,\nand when it does it silently falls back to C: `@property (readonly, nonatomic,\nstrong) NSURLSession *session;` becomes `@` plus a C `function_declarator` named\n`property`. No `property_declaration` node is produced, so every consumer that\nkeys on that node type sees an absence rather than an error.\n\nMEASURED, two routes sharing no code -- `@property` lines in TEXT vs\n`property_declaration` nodes in the TREE:\n\n    repo             files  w/ERROR   @property lines   prop nodes   reached\n    AFNetworking        80       20              245          209     85.3%\n    CocoaLumberjack    224       25              179          132     73.7%\n    fmdb                30       11               51           45     88.2%\n    Mantle              53       18               50           50    100.0%\n\n**72 of 387 files (18.6%) carry a parse ERROR**, and 14.7% / 26.3% / 11.8% of\ndeclared properties never become nodes.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND PROPERTIES. This is an INPUT failure upstream of every\nobjc analysis decision, and it does not announce itself -- `has_error` is True\non the tree and nothing reads it. Three consequences observed on one repository\nin one afternoon:\n\n  1. WI-garar stage 2 types `self.<property>` from its declaration. It works\n     (CocoaLumberjack gained 3 catalogue rows end to end) but its HEADLINE case\n     -- AFNetworking's nine `net_recv` rows, all NSURLSession, all reached via\n     `self.session` -- did NOT move, because `AFURLSessionManager.h` is one of\n     the 20 failing files. A pre-registered, falsifiable prediction was refuted\n     by a defect in a different component.\n  2. `@interface AFURLSessionManager` produces NO class symbol, so\n     `_project_classes` does not contain it, so the emit site's\n     `_declared not in project_classes` guard -- which exists to keep\n     first-party classes out of the module slot -- is silently defeated for\n     exactly the classes whose headers do not parse (40 edges on AFNetworking).\n  3. Any census that counts objc symbols or edges is under-counting by an\n     unknown amount that varies per repository, so an objc measurement's\n     denominator is not trustworthy without checking `has_error` first.\n\nWHAT TO DO FIRST, before any fix: establish WHICH construct breaks the parse.\nThe failing header uses `NS_ASSUME_NONNULL_BEGIN`, `nullable`, and generics; the\ngrammar clearly knows `nullable` (36 nodes in that file), so the trigger is\nnarrower than \"modern ObjC\". Bisect a failing header down to a minimal repro\nbefore deciding whether this is a grammar-version bump, a preprocessing step, or\nan accepted limit that must at least be DISCLOSED.\n\nDISCLOSURE IS THE CHEAP HALF AND MAY BE THE RIGHT FIRST MOVE. `has_error` is\navailable on every tree the analyzer parses and is currently read by nothing. A\nverdict computed over a repository where 18.6% of files failed to parse is a\nclosed-world verdict with an undisclosed hole, which is the shape ADR-0048 and\nthe `unsupported_taint_languages` caveat exist to prevent for whole languages\nand do not currently cover for whole FILES."  # 5085
